WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.700
When you think about enterprise processing power,&nbsp;
you probably think about supercomputers. Or, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.680
might visualize a data center with racks&nbsp;
of servers going from ceiling to floor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.920
But as any mobile user probably knows,&nbsp;
these devices have lots of processing power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.900
This means they can handle the analysis&nbsp;
of data directly. This power shift has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.140
the potential to change the way we&nbsp;
work with technology we use every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.720
In this video, I'll explain an exciting&nbsp;
new emerging capability Samsung and IBM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.640
are working on together to develop the ability&nbsp;
to use containerization--at the edge on Android.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.900
As an example, I'll use a mobile phone. But&nbsp;
what makes processing on the edge so important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.520
Well, it doesn't require data to be sent&nbsp;
across the network to a centralized cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.100
to be analyzed. Instead, it brings applications&nbsp;
closer to the data, which enables faster decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.620
making and smarter data collection. With a&nbsp;
device like this Samsung Galaxy mobile phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.600
edge computing helps us close that last mile&nbsp;
between tech and the people that use it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.849
Modern mobile phones also have several beneficial&nbsp;
functions as edge platforms. That includes high-res&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:16.560
cameras, microphones, accelerometer sensors&nbsp;
and geospatial capabilities. Not to mention they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.780
have significant processing power, 5G connectivity&nbsp;
and advanced security suites like Samsung Knox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.020
So, let's start with an example of how, when&nbsp;
combined with AI, devices on the edge can provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:34.500
rapid results right in someone's hands. Let's&nbsp;
say, for example, I'm a plant safety inspector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.980
My job a lot easier if I could offload routine&nbsp;
inspection work to AI-based visual analysis tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.220
But these visual analysis tools don't have to be&nbsp;
hosted in some faraway data center. That's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:53.520
a really smart phone and camera comes in. In my&nbsp;
job as plant inspector, I can take applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.800
built in the cloud and have them sent directly&nbsp;
to my device from a centralized management tool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.680
So I walk into the plant and for my initial&nbsp;
inspection, start up an app and start recording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:10.620
what I see. I could then immediately apply the AI&nbsp;
model running on my device as I work in the plant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.840
That takes the guesswork out of my role&nbsp;
and uses tech like AI where it can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.700
the biggest difference. Once the inspection is&nbsp;
done, the results can be sent back to the cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.640
That way, they can be stored for historical&nbsp;
purposes and potential model training data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.460
But how do we get our applications out to the&nbsp;
edge and onto the device we use every day in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:39.720
way that is scalable and secure? One answer&nbsp;
is containers. But what is the container in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.560
the first place? And why might we want to run&nbsp;
these on something like this Samsung phone?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.040
In simple language, a container is a single&nbsp;
executable package of software that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.500
an application and all the dependencies it needs&nbsp;
to run. That means the application, its scripts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.780
libraries are all together in one place. So in&nbsp;
order to take advantage of containers on Samsung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.140
devices at the edge, we use the Docker runtime. So&nbsp;
how do containers compare with virtual machines?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.400
With virtual machines, each instance includes&nbsp;
a separate operating system. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.960
containers run of the operating system level.&nbsp;
So they share an operating system kernel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.480
That means containers can spin up really&nbsp;
fast with little overhead per instance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.120
There are other advantages to running containers&nbsp;
on these devices at the edge as well as across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:38.220
cloud. But I'll focus on four main benefits. The&nbsp;
first, easier deployment: Containers are portable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.940
lightweight and easy to distribute through&nbsp;
an orchestration platform like Kubernetes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:48.300
without having to rebuild the core application&nbsp;
each time. Number two, easier development:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.780
Using containers means we can tap into cloud&nbsp;
native developer skills. That means workloads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.440
in AI on the cloud can be moved to the edge&nbsp;
using existing development skills. Number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:08.580
application performance: Because they are sharing&nbsp;
OS level resources across containers in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.200
devices can quickly adjust and maintain&nbsp;
application resource requirements as demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:21.360
rises and declines. And finally, number four:&nbsp;
security. While containers might share the OS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.320
kernel, it appears each container that has&nbsp;
own operating system since it can access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.820
only what was installed within the container&nbsp;
itself. This isolates processes to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.340
what each application has access to and prevent&nbsp;
access to other data or resources on a device.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.760
Now, with lots of applications going to lots of&nbsp;
devices, this could quickly get pretty complex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.660
But luckily we can use software to set policies&nbsp;
to help. So instead of needing someone working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.100
at a particular location to manage which&nbsp;
applications go on which device for which tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:59.520
the IT team can centralize this. With preset&nbsp;
policies, they can automate app management.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.820
In our example, we can set the software policy so&nbsp;
the container with AI application is automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:10.980
sent to the plant safety inspector's mobile&nbsp;
device. With the right models for that plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.420
they're inspecting that day--without&nbsp;
anyone in the plant managing this process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:22.260
This simplifies the management and orchestration&nbsp;
of our applications and helps us to bring tech out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:27.060
of the cloud and onto the devices we use&nbsp;
every day in a scalable and secure way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.940
Thanks for watching. Hey~ Before you leave,&nbsp;
remembered to hit “like” and “subscribe”.

